WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:08.710
Quality is important to me because I think that we all

00:00:08.710 --> 00:00:13.599
have two jobs, the care that we give and improving the systems in which we work.

00:00:13.599 --> 00:00:17.119
That's our professional responsibility.

00:00:17.119 --> 00:00:21.650
I started my career as a nurse, but even before that,

00:00:21.650 --> 00:00:26.770
I'm the oldest of nine children, a big Irish family.

00:00:26.770 --> 00:00:32.980
When the first nephew was born to my sister, you can imagine what this Irish family was

00:00:32.980 --> 00:00:33.980
like.

00:00:33.980 --> 00:00:40.350
We were all responsible together to take care of this wonderful little kid, Robbie.

00:00:40.350 --> 00:00:44.580
When Robbie was two months old, he went to his doctor, had his first checkup,

00:00:44.580 --> 00:00:47.960
and the doctor told my sister that he was great, he was developing normally,

00:00:47.960 --> 00:00:48.960
everything was fine.

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:52.790
At the end of the visit, he gave him his DPT vaccination.

00:00:52.790 --> 00:00:56.070
When my sister got Robbie home, he started to become ill.

00:00:56.070 --> 00:00:59.200
She brought him right back to the office and he ended up

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:02.400
in the intensive care unit for about a week.

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:09.030
He eventually got better, and the whole family came together to make sure that he was safe.

00:01:09.030 --> 00:01:13.460
When he went in for his four month checkup, the doctor said he was perfect, he was developing

00:01:13.460 --> 00:01:16.740
normally, and at the end of the visit, he said to my

00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:19.119
sister, I'm going to give him his DPT vaccination.

00:01:19.119 --> 00:01:23.030
And my sister said, don't you remember what happened the last time?

00:01:23.030 --> 00:01:27.710
He paused for a moment and then my sister recounted what happened

00:01:27.710 --> 00:01:32.540
and he said, that had nothing to do with it.

00:01:32.540 --> 00:01:35.130
But then he paused and he said, I'll give him half a dose,

00:01:35.130 --> 00:01:39.810
and he gave him half a dose, and Robbie died within 24 hours.

00:01:39.810 --> 00:01:45.899
When you think about the story of your nephew, do you think that's an anomaly?

00:01:45.899 --> 00:01:48.520
Do you think that is sort of a rare thing?

00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:52.009
Do you feel like it was indicative of a much broader problem

00:01:52.009 --> 00:01:55.579
or do you think this really was an unusual case that

00:01:55.579 --> 00:01:56.979
got you thinking on this?

00:01:56.979 --> 00:02:00.039
Well, it is not rare.

00:02:00.039 --> 00:02:03.149
Perhaps that particular circumstance, but the fact

00:02:03.149 --> 00:02:07.299
that patients are harmed by their care instead of helped by their carers

00:02:07.299 --> 00:02:12.020
is, unfortunately, all too common.

00:02:12.020 --> 00:02:17.740
So my sister asked me three questions that changed my whole outlook on my career as a

00:02:17.740 --> 00:02:18.740
nurse.

00:02:18.740 --> 00:02:22.260
The first thing she said is, why do you keep records separate?

00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:26.360
Why are the hospital records separate from the doctor's office records?

00:02:26.360 --> 00:02:30.110
The second question she said is, why did he not know that that wasn't the right thing

00:02:30.110 --> 00:02:31.110
to do?

00:02:31.110 --> 00:02:34.310
And then the third thing she said, why didn't he listen to me?

00:02:34.310 --> 00:02:37.890
And those three questions really changed my outlook

00:02:37.890 --> 00:02:41.350
on what we need to improve in quality in health care

00:02:41.350 --> 00:02:45.110
and how we need to go about it.

00:02:45.110 --> 00:02:47.440
In some cases, it's a failure of information.

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:52.180
An example, one of our staff here was taking his father

00:02:52.180 --> 00:02:55.010
home at from one of the hospitals here in Boston

00:02:55.010 --> 00:02:59.730
after having an aneurysm in his brain repaired.

00:02:59.730 --> 00:03:04.970
As the nurse was preparing to discharge his father,

00:03:04.970 --> 00:03:10.870
my staff member went and said to the nurse, do you know what his situation is when he

00:03:10.870 --> 00:03:11.870
gets home?

00:03:11.870 --> 00:03:18.010
The staff member's mother is totally incapacitated and his father was her carer, and the nurse

00:03:18.010 --> 00:03:19.030
had no idea.

00:03:19.030 --> 00:03:22.580
Now, you might not think that that's harm, but I do think it is.

00:03:22.580 --> 00:03:28.330
To send a patient home to a circumstance without complete understanding is harm.

00:03:28.330 --> 00:03:32.260
Patients developing pressure ulcers in a hospital is harm.

00:03:32.260 --> 00:03:35.880
Ventilator pneumonias are eliminatable now.

00:03:35.880 --> 00:03:40.330
And so I think these all represent professional challenges to us

00:03:40.330 --> 00:03:42.610
because we do know how to eliminate these.

00:03:42.610 --> 00:03:45.430
So what's the challenge?

00:03:45.430 --> 00:03:46.440
Why does this happen?

00:03:46.440 --> 00:03:48.400
Why do these harms occur?

00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:49.400
What's underlying it?

00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:50.920
Well, I'd say two things.

00:03:50.920 --> 00:03:55.120
One is in our professional education, we're taught how to care,

00:03:55.120 --> 00:03:56.510
how to fix things.

00:03:56.510 --> 00:03:59.160
We're not taught how to improve.

00:03:59.160 --> 00:04:05.680
Several years ago here at IHI, we were trying to get improvement built into the curricula

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:09.430
for medical schools, nursing schools, health care administration programs.

00:04:09.430 --> 00:04:14.390
We found it very hard to get people to integrate that curriculum

00:04:14.390 --> 00:04:17.289
and to teach cross-professionally.

00:04:17.289 --> 00:04:20.850
The second problem is we work in broken systems.

00:04:20.850 --> 00:04:24.590
In our educational processes, we're taught to be heroes.

00:04:24.590 --> 00:04:29.360
We're taught to fix it for the individual, not to fix the process and the system.

00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:30.610
So two things, I think.

00:04:30.610 --> 00:04:34.400
We need to build improvement capability, the science of improvement,

00:04:34.400 --> 00:04:38.520
into every curriculum, into every professional program.

00:04:38.520 --> 00:04:40.710
And then we have to be able to see.

00:04:40.710 --> 00:04:45.139
We have to learn that when we're in a situation, not to fix, but to see.

00:04:45.139 --> 00:04:48.150
And once we see the system, we can make it better for all.

00:04:48.150 --> 00:04:50.120
Those are two great points.

00:04:50.120 --> 00:04:55.520
And certainly, as a clinician, the notion of improvement as a part of my job is not

00:04:55.520 --> 00:04:59.780
something I spend a lot of time thinking about, and I suspect most clinicians not very much

00:04:59.780 --> 00:05:00.780
time.

00:05:00.780 --> 00:05:03.700
And then the second notion that you bring up, very much we

00:05:03.700 --> 00:05:06.860
think about fixing it for individual patients because that's our job,

00:05:06.860 --> 00:05:11.750
but never taking the step back and saying, how do we fix this, not just for my patient

00:05:11.750 --> 00:05:14.250
in front of me, but the patient I might have a month from

00:05:14.250 --> 00:05:16.619
now, or your patient that I will never see.

